# Multiple Environments
# Switch between development, staging, and production without changing code
# Uses @env directive to load named .env files

### Example setup:
# Your workspace should have .env files:
#   .env           (default)
#   dev.env
#   staging.env
#   prod.env
#
# Each file defines environment variables like:
#   API_URL=https://api.dev.example.com
#   API_KEY=dev-key-123
#   DATABASE_URL=postgres://localhost/dev_db

### Default environment (.env file)
# No @env directive = loads .env file automatically
@baseUrl = https://api.example.com

###

GET {{baseUrl}}/users

###

### Development environment (dev.env)
# Change the comment to switch to dev environment
# @env = dev

# This loads dev.env instead of .env
# With @env = dev, variables come from:
# 1. System environment variables (highest priority)
# 2. dev.env file
# 3. File variables like @baseUrl above

###

GET https://api.dev.example.com/users

###

### Staging environment (staging.env)
# Uncomment to use staging
# @env = staging

###

GET https://api.staging.example.com/users

###

### Production environment (prod.env)
# Uncomment to use production (BE CAREFUL!)
# @env = prod

###

GET https://api.prod.example.com/users

###

### Using environment variables from files
@env = dev

# If dev.env contains:
#   API_URL=https://api.dev.example.com
#   API_KEY=dev-key-123
#
# You can use them like this:

GET {{API_URL}}/users
Authorization: Bearer {{API_KEY}}

###

### Switching environments per request
# You can't change @env mid-file, but you can:
# 1. Create separate files (api-dev.http, api-prod.http)
# 2. Or create separate example files with different @env

# Example: create api.http with @env = dev
# Then when running from CLI: dot-http --env prod api.http
# This overrides the @env directive in the file

###

### Variable precedence with @env
# Highest to lowest:
# 1. System environment variables (set in OS)
# 2. Named .env file (dev.env, prod.env, etc. if @env specified)
# 3. Default .env file (if no @env or system/override doesn't define var)
# 4. File variables (@var = value)

# Example: if both .env and dev.env define API_KEY
# And you use @env = dev
# Then dev.env's API_KEY is used (it's in a named file)

###

### Recommended .env file structure:

# .env (default/development)
# API_URL=http://localhost:3000
# API_KEY=dev-key-insecure
# DATABASE_URL=postgres://localhost/dev_db

# dev.env
# API_URL=https://api.dev.example.com
# API_KEY=dev-key-123
# DATABASE_URL=postgres://dev.example.com/dev_db

# staging.env
# API_URL=https://api.staging.example.com
# API_KEY=staging-key-456
# DATABASE_URL=postgres://staging.example.com/staging_db

# prod.env (NEVER commit this to version control!)
# API_URL=https://api.example.com
# API_KEY=prod-key-789
# DATABASE_URL=postgres://prod.example.com/prod_db

###

### CLI usage (for Go CLI tool):
# dot-http api.http                  # uses .env
# dot-http --env dev api.http        # loads dev.env
# dot-http --env staging api.http    # loads staging.env
# dot-http --env prod api.http       # loads prod.env
